Q: How to preserve cursor for ODBC under Informix after SQLEndTran A: <p>The most reliable solution is combining SQL_ATTR_CURSOR_HOLD and WITH HOLD in the SQL statement. The WITH HOLD clause is native Informix SQL syntax and is the most portable solution across different ODBC driver versions:</p>
<pre><code>SQLHSTMT hstmt = SQL_NULL_HSTMT;

SQLAllocHandle(SQL_HANDLE_STMT, hdbc, &amp;hstmt);

// Enable cursor hold BEFORE preparing/executing
SQLSetStmtAttr(hstmt, SQL_ATTR_CURSOR_HOLD, (SQLPOINTER)SQL_CURSOR_HOLD_ON, 0);

// Or use WITH HOLD in SQL:
SQLPrepare(hstmt, (SQLCHAR*)&quot;SELECT * FROM tab1 FOR READ ONLY WITH HOLD&quot;, SQL_NTS);
SQLExecute(hstmt);

SQLFetch(hstmt);
SQLFetch(hstmt);

SQLSetConnectAttr(hdbc, SQL_ATTR_AUTOCOMMIT, (SQLPOINTER)SQL_AUTOCOMMIT_OFF, 0);

/* Do the work here */

SQLEndTran(SQL_HANDLE_DBC, hdbc, SQL_COMMIT);

SQLSetConnectAttr(hdbc, SQL_ATTR_AUTOCOMMIT, (SQLPOINTER)SQL_AUTOCOMMIT_ON, 0);

// This should work:
SQLFetch(hstmt);
</code></pre>
<p>For unixODBC headers use WITH HOLD:</p>
<pre><code>// Declare cursor with WITH HOLD in SELECT statement
SQLPrepare(hstmt, (SQLCHAR*)&quot;SELECT * FROM tab1 FOR READ ONLY WITH HOLD&quot;, SQL_NTS);
SQLExecute(hstmt);
// Fetch some rows
SQLFetch(hstmt);
SQLFetch(hstmt);
// Commit transaction - cursor should remain open
SQLEndTran(SQL_HANDLE_DBC, hdbc, SQL_COMMIT);
// Continue fetching - should work with WITH HOLD
SQLFetch(hstmt);
</code></pre>
<p>This is bit older but could be useful for examples:
<a href="https://github.com/OpenInformix/ODBCExamples" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/OpenInformix/ODBCExamples</a></p>
<p>Exact values from IBM documentation:</p>
<pre><code>/* Informix-specific ODBC extensions (from infxcli.h) */
/* Include these defines in your source if infxcli.h is not available */
#ifndef SQL_ATTR_CURSOR_HOLD
#define SQL_ATTR_CURSOR_HOLD         1250  /* Verify in your infxcli.h */
#endif
#ifndef SQL_CURSOR_HOLD_ON
#define SQL_CURSOR_HOLD_ON           1     /* Default - cursor preserved after COMMIT */
#endif
#ifndef SQL_CURSOR_HOLD_OFF
#define SQL_CURSOR_HOLD_OFF          0     /* Cursor closed after COMMIT */
#endif
</code></pre>
